 succeeded in 332ms:
228-- `Packet compliance report` when packet-floor doctrine or omission findings are the live surface
229-- `Context sufficiency review memo` or `Critique quality review memo` when weak context or no-gain iteration may explain benchmark instability
230-
231-Keep these maintenance control families direct in the runtime surface:
232:- runtime owner integrity
233:- chapter-family answerability
234-- document-benchmark to assembled-replay escalation
235-- route choice between staying document-grounded vs escalating to behavior proof
236-
237-Default route policy for this maintenance class:
238:- primary route: stay at document-level benchmark while the live question is doc/runtime parity, chapter-family answerability, or benchmark coverage
239-- fallback route: escalate to assembled replay when ambiguity, conflict, or behavior-critical claims need stronger evidence than document-grounded review can provide
240-- final ownership reroutes to `eval-ops` once the decision depends on replay execution state, benchmark gating, or release-sensitive continuation rather than route comparison alone
241-
242-Direct family note:
243:- for `runtime owner integrity` and `benchmark-loop adequacy`, document-level benchmark is the primary sufficient surface when runtime-owned docs directly expose the owner boundary or benchmark loop
244:- replay escalation is not required for those families unless the claim extends from document/runtime parity into assembled behavior proof or runner execution state
245-
246-Escalate to stronger operational artifacts when:
247-- document parity is no longer the live question and assembled prompt behavior must be replayed
248-- scenario lineage, run linkage, or replay execution state must stay inspectable across reruns
--
336-- Guardrails / Safety -> `PROMPT_guardrails_safety_overlay`
337-- Evaluation / Monitoring -> `PROMPT_evaluation_monitoring_overlay` + `eval-ops`; prefer `Benchmark registry memo`, `Guide reflection benchmark memo`, or `Prompt-stack release review`
338-
339-Lookup rule:
340:- if a chapter family can only be answered by operator-facing prose and not by the active runtime / skill surface, treat that as a reflection gap and patch the actual prompt docs before claiming parity
341-
342-Packet rule:
343-- one packet should solve one control problem
344-- if packet overhead exceeds its decision value, simplify or drop it
--
347-- if the required packet exists but the recommended companion is missing, keep the narrower companion-missing language rather than full-strength claim language
348-- if future behavior or reusable defaults are being changed, pair `Learning-signal review memo` with `Adaptation decision memo` rather than silently mutating defaults
349-- if async or delegated work remains active, prefer lifecycle packets over prose-only status narration
350-- host-runtime maintenance and release reviews should still be able to see whether packet families cover the active control boundaries
351:- keep guide / runtime / skill lookup parity visible for goal, recovery, approval, budget, prioritization, readiness, and lifecycle-audit packets; do not let one layer silently know a family the others no longer expose
352-- keep coding-briefing, research-transparency, resource-concurrency, packet-compliance, replay-review, release-evidence, benchmark-registry, context-sufficiency, critique-quality, adaptation-promotion, and route-quality packets equally visible when those are the live control boundaries
353-- keep benchmark-execution, replay-verdict, context-failure, critique-utility, adaptation-lifecycle, route-reprioritization, coding-proof, release-v2, and telemetry-trend packets equally visible when those are the live control boundaries
354-- packet presence is not operational proof; if execution, controller behavior, or promotion evidence is the live question, choose the artifact that actually carries the linked evidence
355-- distinguish `light review memo`, `stronger packet`, and `operational artifact` rather than treating every packet as equal-strength evidence
